    "updated_at": "2026-06-07 03:02:08",
    "episode_evidence": "The South Korean memory chip industry, led by giants like SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics, is at the center of a speculative bubble, as indicated by the staggering profit projections reaching $200 billion by 2028, a figure that seems unsustainable given the competitive landscape. 'I do think it is a bubble because the estimates for profits... we're now talking 200 billion US dollars in that profit estimates for 2028,' Michael Fritzell points out. This valuation is further threatened by the rise of Chinese competitors, who are expected to ramp up production significantly by 2027, potentially disrupting the market and impacting South Korean firms' profit margins. 'These Chinese competitors... are now very profitable. And they will come with new supply in 2027 onwards,' Fritzell warns. Meanwhile, Southeast Asia, particularly Thailand and the Philippines, offers a compelling contrast, with small-cap stocks trading at low multiples, presenting an opportunity for value investors seeking undervalued assets. 'You can find single-digit P stocks, like solid quality companies, growing companies are at single-digit P ratios,' Fritzell notes.",
    "falsification_tracks": [
      "If SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics fail to meet or consistently miss their profit projections over the next few quarters, it would cast doubt on the sustainability of their current valuations.",
      "Should Chinese memory chip manufacturers delay their production ramp-up or face significant operational challenges, the competitive threat to South Korean firms would be reduced.",
      "A sudden increase in global memory chip demand could alleviate concerns about overvaluation in the South Korean market by supporting higher profit margins.",
      "If Southeast Asian markets experience a sharp economic downturn or political instability, the attractiveness of their undervalued small-cap stocks could diminish."
    ],
