    "updated_at": "2026-07-24 03:02:22",
    "episode_evidence": "Eiso Kant's journey into AI was sparked by Andrej Karpathy's article on the Unreasonable Effectiveness of Recurrent Airlines, which he stumbled upon in 2015. 'I pivoted my startup at the time overnight to working on RNNs and later LSTM's and transformer models to be able to write code,' he recalls. This pivotal moment led him to found a fully open-source company focused on machine learning on code. However, during those early years, 'no one cared,' highlighting the significant challenge of gaining traction for such innovative work. Kant's company spent 'about four or five years' on this endeavor until the end of 2019, which he later considered 'the biggest failure of my career,' having spent $12 million of investors' money. Despite this setback, Kant's belief in the potential of smaller AI models to outperform larger ones and the importance of open-source contributions remained strong, eventually leading to the creation of Poolside AI, which focuses on democratizing AI and scaling language models.",
    "falsification_tracks": [
      "If the number of active contributors to Poolside AI's open-source projects declines significantly over the next year, it could indicate a waning interest in the democratization of AI.",
      "Should major tech companies like Google or OpenAI release breakthroughs in AI that render Poolside AI's models obsolete or less competitive, it would challenge the thesis of the importance of scaling in AI development.",
      "If Poolside AI fails to secure further funding or partnerships, it could signal that the market does not value the open-source approach to AI development as highly as suggested."
    ],
    "insight_title": "Inside the Model Factory \u2014 Eiso Kant, Poolside AI",
